{{ if extra.code_conventions }}

{{ extra.code_conventions }}
{{ /if }}
{{ if node }}

# Libraries to use

- `ayy` to assert things (when it's okay to throw `AssertionError` instead of a domain-specific error).
	- Always use the most specific assertion function, so that the error message shows the unexpected value when possible.
		- e.g. `A.gte(x, 1); A.lte(x, 3)` instead of `A(x >= 1 && x <= 3);`
- `logtape` for logging. Logs teach us about anomalies and the causes of things; log what a human operator would probably be interested in when observing the system.
{{ if effection }}
- `effection`. We like structured programming and async/await is lacking; prefer to write an Operation instead of a Promise-returning function (unless needed).
{{ /if }}
{{ for library of libraries_extra }}
- {{ library }}
{{ /for }}
- `vitest` for unit tests. Writing more tests is fine.
- `fast-check` for property-based testing where needed, i.e. to check a bunch of variations on a string or number.
{{ /if }}
{{ if web_design == "2010" }}

# Web design

Set your mind to 2010 styling and information density; rely almost entirely on lightly styled (not referring to color) native HTML elements.

{{ if color_scheme == "dark" }}
This is a "dark mode"-only project, so text should be light and backgrounds should be dark.
{{ else if color_scheme == "light" }}
This is a "light mode"-only project, so text should be black and backgrounds should be light.
{{ else }}
Please keep things working well in both light mode and dark mode.
{{ /if }}

Write CSS mostly the old way and don't add more than you really need.

It's okay to make things look nice within these constraints, like using 8px padding or grid, suitable line-height, color or font-weight or font-family for differentiation, etc.

We really want things to be obvious at a glance while not having superfluous stuff.

Also, no new title= tooltips; they're dubious desktop-only things which must be written by humans.
